  <h3>StartPhase</h3>
  <p>
    <code>spin/phases/StartPhase.ts</code>, name <code>start</code>, skippable and quickenable.
    Config <code>{'{ spinningMode, delay? }'}</code>. <code>onEnter</code> sets
    <code>reel.spinningMode</code>, calls <code>haltDrive()</code> and runs the default steps,
    edited by <code>options.steps</code> if the phase was registered with one. <code>delay</code>
    waits <code>step[0].duration ?? config.delay ?? 0</code>. <code>pull</code> only exists when
    <code>timing.bounceDistance &gt; 0</code>: speed goes to <code>PULL_SPEED = -2</code> over
    <code>step[0].duration ?? 50</code> ms with <code>step[0].ease ?? 'power1.out'</code> (under
    the drive model it sets <code>targetSpeed = -2</code> and waits). <code>accelerate</code>
    goes to <code>timing.spinSpeed</code> over <code>accelerationDuration ?? 300</code> ms with
    <code>accelerationEase ?? 'power2.in'</code>. <code>onSkip</code> calls
    <code>forceSpeed(spinSpeed)</code> and <code>notifySpinStart()</code> (idempotent), and under
    a quicken also <code>_complete()</code>, because that pose is already the natural end.
  </p>
  <h3>SpinPhase</h3>
  <p>
    <code>SpinPhase.ts</code>, name <code>spin</code>, <em>not</em> skippable, quickenable.
    <code>_minTime = config.minimumSpinTime ?? timing.minimumSpinTime ?? 500</code>.
    <code>resolve()</code> is public and only the controller calls it. A slam does nothing in
    <code>onSkip</code> (the controller uses <code>forceComplete</code>); a quicken sets
    <code>_minTime = 0</code> and completes if the reel is already ready to stop. It fires
    nothing beyond <code>phase:enter</code> and <code>phase:exit</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>StopPhase</h3>
  <p>
    <code>StopPhase.ts</code>, name <code>stop</code>, skippable and quickenable. Config
    <code>{'{ targetFrame, delay?, preserveSpeed? }'}</code>. <code>_stage</code> moves through
    <code>delay</code>, <code>spinning</code>, <code>landed</code>, <code>done</code>;
    <code>_baseY</code> records the container's main-axis position on enter. During
    <code>delay</code> the reel keeps its current speed, which after a tease is the slow tease
    speed. <code>spinOut</code> calls <code>setStopFrame(targetFrame)</code>, which loads the
    full frame (buffers included) into the stop queue and sets <code>isStopping</code>; with
    <code>preserveSpeed</code> the speed becomes <code>min(max(reel.speed, spinSpeed * 0.08), spinSpeed)</code>,
    otherwise it returns to <code>spinSpeed</code>. The step then waits
    <code>until(!stopSequencer.hasRemaining)</code>: each time a symbol wraps off the strip the
    next frame symbol is pulled in. <code>land</code> calls <code>haltDrive()</code> (never
    <code>speed = 0</code>, which a drive would ramp back up), <code>snapToGrid()</code>,
    <code>notifySpinEnd()</code> and <code>notifyLanded()</code>, which raises
    <code>landing</code>. <code>bounce</code> is a two-leg timeline, out to
    <code>base + bounceDistance</code> in half the time and back in the other half, ease
    <code>bounceEase ?? 'power1.out'</code>; a distance of 0 returns an already-settled bounce.
    The slam path in <code>onSkip</code> halts the drive, calls <code>placeStrip(targetFrame)</code>,
    resets the container to <code>_baseY</code> <em>before</em> <code>snapToGrid</code> (snapping
    mid-bounce used to lock in the overshoot), and sets <code>_stage = 'done'</code>.
  </p>
  <h3>AdjustPhase and the cascade phases</h3>
  <p>
    <code>AdjustPhase.ts</code>, name <code>adjust</code>, MultiWays only. The builder registers it
    with <code>pinMigrationDuration</code> (default 200 ms) and <code>pinMigrationEase</code>
    (default <code>'power2.out'</code>). The reshape geometry is already applied by
    <code>_applyReshape</code> before the phase runs; it only tweens pin overlays from their old
    position and scale to the new one, snapping when <code>durationMs &lt;= 0</code>. The three
    cascade phases (<code>cascade:fall</code>, <code>cascade:place</code>,
    <code>cascade:dropIn</code>) are all skippable and quickenable, and their slam pose is their
    natural end, so a quicken completes them. Defaults: fall 300 ms <code>'sine.in'</code>,
    drop-in 600 ms <code>'power2.out'</code> with a 60 ms cell stagger. They are covered on the
    <a href="/architecture/cascade/">cascade page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>ReelPhase: the base class</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ReelPhase.ts</code> declares <code>abstract class ReelPhase&lt;TConfig, TProfile&gt;</code>
    with abstract <code>name</code>, <code>skippable</code>, <code>update(deltaMs)</code>,
    <code>onEnter(config)</code> and <code>onSkip(ctx)</code>; <code>quickenable</code> defaults to
    false. Getters: <code>reel</code>, <code>speed</code> (the raw profile, which a quicken can
    replace), <code>timing</code> (<code>resolvePhaseProfile(speed, context.phase || name, context.anticipated)</code>),
    <code>context</code>, <code>anticipated</code>, <code>isActive</code>, <code>quickened</code>.
    <code>PhaseCreateContext</code> is <code>{'{ phase, reelIndex, anticipated, quickened }'}</code>,
    where <code>anticipated</code> means the reel played a tease to its end earlier this spin.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>run(config)</code> sets <code>_isActive</code>, stores the config, applies any primed quicken, emits <code>phase:enter</code> and returns a promise whose resolver emits <code>phase:exit</code> first. Then it calls <code>onEnter(config)</code>.</li>
    <li><code>_complete()</code> cancels steps and resolves once. This is how control passes on: the controller's <code>await phase.run()</code> returns and the chain creates the next phase. Phases never create their successor.</li>
    <li><code>skip(ctx)</code> does nothing if inactive. Quicken: swaps in <code>ctx.speed</code> if given, sets <code>_quickened</code>, calls <code>onSkip</code> only if <code>quickenable</code>, then <code>_quickenSteps()</code>; it does not complete the phase itself. Slam: only if <code>skippable</code>, <code>_cancelSteps()</code>, <code>onSkip(ctx)</code>, <code>_complete()</code>.</li>
    <li><code>forceComplete(ctx)</code> is a slam that ignores <code>skippable</code>. The controller's slam path uses it.</li>
    <li><code>primeQuicken(ctx)</code> marks a phase created after the press, so its run starts quickened and <code>cut</code> steps are skipped from the first one.</li>
    <li><code>attachContext(ctx)</code> is called once by <code>PhaseFactory.create</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The steps system</h2>
  <p>
    <code>spin/phases/steps.ts</code>. A <code>PhaseStep</code> is <code>{'{ name, run(ctx), cut? }'}</code>,
    built with <code>step(name, run, {'{ cut }'})</code>. <code>run</code> returns a
    <code>StepResult</code>: a GSAP animation (killed on cancel), a promise (stopped through
    <code>ctx.signal</code>), a <code>Cancellable {'{ done, cancel }'}</code> such as a bounce, or
    nothing for an instant step. The list editors <code>insertBefore(steps, name, ...added)</code>,
    <code>insertAfter</code>, <code>replaceStep(steps, name, replacement)</code> and
    <code>removeStep(steps, name)</code> each return a copy and throw on an unknown name, listing
    the names that exist. A <code>StepsEditor</code> is <code>(steps) =&gt; steps</code>, passed as
    <code>register('stop', StopPhase, {'{ steps }'})</code>. <code>runStep(run, ctx, controller?)</code>
    is public and returns <code>{'{ done, settled, cancel }'}</code>; <code>settled</code> lets instant
    steps chain in the same call. <code>runSteps</code> warns once if the profile configures a
    step name the list does not have, cancels any previous run, and calls <code>_complete()</code>
    after the last step.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mb-1">StepContext</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>reel</code>, <code>container</code>, <code>main</code> (<code>'x' | 'y'</code>), <code>gsap</code></li>
        <li><code>profile</code>: the phase's <code>timing</code></li>
        <li><code>step: readonly StepTiming[]</code>: this step's entries from <code>profile[phase].steps[name]</code>, with <code>whenAnticipated</code> merged over; built-ins read <code>step[0]</code></li>
        <li><code>anticipated</code>, <code>quickened</code>, <code>config</code>, <code>phase</code>, <code>signal</code></li>
        <li><code>wait(ms)</code>: a GSAP <code>delayedCall</code> that resolves early on abort</li>
        <li><code>until(predicate)</code>: checked from <code>tickSteps()</code> each update</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-4">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mb-1">phase:step statuses</div>
      <ul class="text-sm space-y-1 text-foreground/80">
        <li><code>start</code>, <code>end</code>: the step ran normally</li>
        <li><code>skipped</code>: a <code>cut</code> step a quicken never started</li>
        <li><code>cut</code>: a <code>cut</code> step a quicken stopped while it was running</li>
        <li><code>cancelled</code>: stopped by a slam or by <code>destroy()</code></li>
        <li>payload <code>{'{ phase, step, status }'}</code> on <code>reel.events</code>; <code>phase:enter</code> and <code>phase:exit</code> wrap the whole phase</li>
        <li><code>StepTiming</code> is <code>{'{ ease?, duration? }'}</code></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>PhaseFactory and registration order</h2>
  <p>
    <code>PhaseFactory.ts</code> pre-registers <code>start</code>, <code>spin</code>,
    <code>stop</code> and <code>anticipation</code> in its constructor.
    <code>register(name, PhaseClass, options?)</code> stores
    <code>(reel, speed) =&gt; new PhaseClass(reel, speed, options)</code>, with the options type
    read from the constructor's third parameter. <code>registerFactory(name, (reel, speed, ctx) =&gt; phase)</code>
    is for phases that need extra arguments or should be chosen per reel or per tease state.
    <code>create(name, reel, speed, context)</code> throws with the registered names if
    <code>name</code> is unknown, then calls <code>attachContext</code>. <code>has(name)</code>
    backs <code>resolveSpinMode</code> and <code>refill</code>. The builder wires things in this
    order: the four built-ins, then <code>.tumble()</code> registers the three cascade factories,
    then MultiWays registers <code>adjust</code>, and your <code>.phases(f =&gt; ...)</code>
    configurators run last so they can replace any default.
  </p>

  <h2>Spinning modes</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>SpinningMode</code> (<code>spin/modes/</code>) only answers one question:
    <code>computeDelta(slotPitch, speed, deltaMs)</code>, the main-axis movement in pixels for
    this frame. It is consulted only in <code>Reel.update</code>; <code>StartPhase</code> and
    <code>CascadeFallPhase</code> assign it to the reel and the builder picks it with
    <code>.spinningMode()</code>. <code>StandardMode</code> computes
    <code>slotPitch * speed * deltaMs / 1000</code> capped at half a cell per frame, and lets a
    negative value through for the start pull. <code>CascadeMode</code> multiplies by gravity
    (default 1.5), caps at one cell and never goes negative. <code>ImmediateMode</code> always
    returns 0 and leaves placement to the phase. Landing, frames and timing belong to the phases
    and the controller, never to a mode. One thing to note from reading the code: the profile
    documents <code>spinSpeed</code> as pixels per frame, while <code>StandardMode</code> treats it
    as cells per second with that half-cell cap; the <a href="/architecture/motion/">motion page</a>
    has the pixel arithmetic.
  </p>

  <h2>How the promise resolves</h2>
  <p>
    <code>_markLanded(i)</code> is idempotent and runs in this order: add the reel to
    <code>_landedReels</code>; resolve its landing promise (which releases
    <code>'sequential'</code> stagger and group barriers); emit <code>anticipation:reelEnd</code>
    if the reel was teasing; emit the reel's <code>landed</code> and then the set-level
    <code>spin:reelLanded(i, symbols)</code>; settle any finished quicken batch; and once every
    non-held reel has landed, call <code>_finishSpin()</code>. On the abort path that rejects
    with <code>_pendingAbortError</code> and emits no success events. On the normal path it
    builds the result, clears state, calls <code>hooks.clearTargetShape()</code>, emits
    <code>spin:allLanded(result)</code> then <code>spin:complete(result)</code>, and resolves.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-card overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-muted/60">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">SpinResult field</th>
            <th class="text-left px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">Value</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            ['symbols', 'string[][], one column per reel'],
            ['wasSkipped', 'true after any slam or quicken, including the error-recovery slam'],
            ['skipMode', "'slam' | 'quicken' | null"],
            ['skipContext', "SkipContext | null; { mode: 'slam' } for engine slams"],
            ['duration', 'ms since spin()'],
          ].map(([k, v]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-border/60">
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-mono text-primary text-xs">{k}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-xs text-foreground/80">{v}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    The watchdog: <code>options.signal</code> rejects with <code>signal.reason</code>, and
    <code>options.timeoutMs</code> (off by default, values of 0 or less ignored) rejects on
    expiry. Both route to <code>_abortSpin</code>, which sets <code>_pendingAbortError</code> and
    slams the reels to a clean grid through <code>abortSlam()</code>. Refills have no watchdog and
    no reject path.
  </p>

